48 pieces of clothing made up 40% of the total, so 100% - 40% = \boxed{60%} of the clothing remained. Since Roger split the remaining into 9 equal loads, we can find the number of pieces in each load by dividing the remaining number of pieces by the number of loads.

\boxed{\frac{120 - 48}{9}} = \boxed{\frac{72}{9}} = \boxed{8}

So, each of the small loads had 8 pieces of clothing.

